2024-29, Laws of Florida, government-owned fire department vehicles other than fire patrol or volunteer fire department vehicles, with a gross vehicle weight rating over 24,000 pounds, may display blue if the fire chief authorizes it in writing and the blue is shown only on the rear.\"\n      \t}\n    \t},\n    \t{\n      \t\"@type\": \"Question\",\n      \t\"name\": \"Are blue lights still police-only in Florida?\",\n      \t\"acceptedAnswer\": {\n        \t\"@type\": \"Answer\",\n        \t\"text\": \"No. That was accurate before the 2024 amendment. Section 316.2397(2) now reads as a general prohibition with an exception list covering police vehicles, qualifying government fire department vehicles, and Department of Corrections or county correctional agency vehicles responding to emergencies.\"\n      \t}\n    \t},\n    \t{\n      \t\"@type\": \"Question\",\n      \t\"name\": \"What happens if a Florida volunteer firefighter misuses red lights?\",\n      \t\"acceptedAnswer\": {\n        \t\"@type\": \"Answer\",\n        \t\"text\": \"Florida Statute § 316.2398(5) provides that a member of a volunteer fire department who uses red lights in violation of that section shall be dismissed from the department.\"\n      \t}\n    \t},\n    \t{\n      \t\"@type\": \"Question\",\n      \t\"name\": \"When can a Florida wrecker use amber lights?\",\n      \t\"acceptedAnswer\": {\n        \t\"@type\": \"Answer\",\n        \t\"text\": \"Under § 316.2397(3)(e), when in actual operation or when a hazard exists. The statute specifies the lights are not to be used going to and from the scene.\"\n      \t}\n    \t}\n  \t]\n\t},\n\t{\n  \t\"@type\": \"Organization\",\n  \t\"@id\": \"https://strobesnmore.com/#organization\",\n  \t\"name\": \"Strobes N' More\",\n  \t\"url\": \"https://strobesnmore.com/\",\n  \t\"telephone\": \"+1-401-454-4487\",\n  \t\"email\": \"sales@strobesnmore.com\",\n  \t\"address\": {\n    \t\"@type\": \"PostalAddress\",\n    \t\"streetAddress\": \"81 Tom Harvey 